  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nOur approach fundamentally changes the representation of the step function by viewing it as a composition of elementary wavelets. Instead of optimizing over arbitrary continuous values, we represent h(x) as a weighted sum of specific basis functions that minimize pairwise overlaps through their Fourier components. This leverages the Hardy-Littlewood circle theorem to guarantee minimal overlaps between shifted functions. We use a combination of Lanczos sigma factor for boundary smoothing and complex exponential basis functions with optimally matched frequencies and phases.\n\nThe central idea is to find weights for these basis functions that minimize the worst-case overlap across all shifts. This is inspired by extremal problems in harmonic analysis where functions with periodic components are known to achieve minimal correlations. The approach uses the following key insights:\n\n- The whitest functions (maximal spectral gap) tend to minimize correlations\n- Negative frequencies can be used to create counteracting correlations\n- The optimization is over invariants of the complex exponential family\n\nWe start with a small set of superpositions (4 frequencies) and allow them to interfere constructively and destructively. The representation ensures mathematical bounds on the overlap by controlling the maximum spectral amplitude at any frequency bin.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\nWe represent the function h(x) as:\n\nh(x) = Re[ sum_{k=1}^{m} a_k * exp(2\u03c0 i b_k x) ] + c * x + d\n\nwhere:\n- a_k are complex coefficients (real and imaginary parts)\n- b_k are the frequencies chosen from a pseudo-random set\n- Re is the real part of a complex number\n- c and d are real number boundary terms\n\nThe representation ensures that h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x by:\n1. Applying Clenshaw-Curtis quadrature to enforce the boundary conditions\n2. Projecting the function onto the sphere of unit norm in the L\u00b2 space\n3. Using a fixed-point iteration to enforce the integral constraint\n\nThe optimization objective is:\n\nmin_{a,b,c,d} max_{k} | (h(x) * (1 - h(x+k))) * dx |\n\nwhere the maximum is taken over all shifts k in [-N,N]. This is formulated as a difficult convex-concave saddle-point problem. \n\nWe use projected gradient descent with automatic derivative calculation from Fast Fourier Transforms (FFT). The gradient is computed as:\n\n\u2207J = (potential function) \u00d7 (spectral correlation matrix)\n\nwhere \u00d7 denotes the outer product when dealing with complex coefficients.\n\nThe optimization is subject to the following constraints:\n\n|h(x)| \u2264 1 for all x\nintegral h(x) dx = 1\nPhase constraints: Im(h(x)) = 0\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nWe define helper functions for:\n\na. Derivative projection: projects the function onto [0,1] with minimal distortion\ndef function(x, h, n):\n    return h[int(x*n)]]   # piecewise constant interpolation\n\nb. New objective function that incorporates:\n\n- Chebyshev economization of shifted exponential functions\n- Fast Fourier Transform-based correlation computation\n- analytic bounds for frequencies to accelerate optimization\n\nc. Initial population generation:\npopulation = []\nfor i in range(10):\n    a = np.random.randn(4) + 1+np.random.rand(4)*0.5\n    b = np.random.rand(4)*10\n    d = 0.5 + (np.random.rand(1)-0.5)*0.1\n    h = compute_from_coeffs(a,b,d)\n    h = project_to_feasible(h)\n    population.append(h)\n\nd. Optimization loop:\n\nWith population:\n1. Compute fitness = C5 for each candidate\n2. Sort by fitness\n3. Select parents with tournament selection\n4. Apply mutation using Chebyshev mapping\n5. Apply crossover in the spectral domain\n6. Project offspring to feasible region\n7. Elite the best solution\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\n- Primary variation: 4-frequency representation\n- Secondary variation: Increase representation size to 6 frequencies\n- Termination variation: Use Bayesian optimization for epsilon stopping\n- Boundary handling: Use Zermelo navigation domain for avoiding constraints\n\nEach variation is applied if time > 90% budget AND diversity > 0.5\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\n- n_points=1600 requires ~4.2e6 operations per generation\n- 50 generations budgeted, with 5-10 seconds per genotype evaluation\n- Use early kill for stalling population\n- Vectorize FFT computations using scipy.fft\n- Check for NaN/Inf during population evaluation\n- Maintain diversity parameter based on pairwise Simpson descriptors\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\n- Check final candidate passes all constraints\n- Re-evaluate using the provided correlation metric\n- Ensure c5_value < 0.3808 if possible\n- Return the best candidate and its score",
